for a week now thousands have come out  into the square in Tripoli to join the  chorus of voices across the country  calling for their government to go as  all eyes were on Beirut a few nights ago  this video posted online went viral  dance music played to a jubilant crowd  many first thought this was Beirut when  it was actually here in Tripoli the city  many see is far more conservative  I didn't plan for this I didn't plan for  this I was like all the people I was  going to like all the people we met up  with a man behind the music he says he  played it spontaneously didn't expect  this kind of reaction all the people  they all loved each other  Sydney there's the Messi hey Christian  everything everything everyone everyone  is here because there is no one on top  of them the protests have mobilized the  youth here a leaderless grassroots  movement that has inspired many it was  like a domino effect among the youth in  Tripoli Sally says the protests have  given her renewed hope I asked her  whether she thinks there are challenges  ahead it's gonna be difficult we are  scared and we are aware that we are  going to a bit of a chaotic period after  this but when you're aware it means  you're ready and when you're ready if  this is the first step of succeeding  you're when you're scared you either do  two things you either withdraw or this  gives you perseverance to continue  especially when you got strong out of  many disappointments tripoli is  traditionally a support base for the  prime minister Saad Hariri  but the Sunni majority city has been  neglected it has some of the poorest  areas in the country we are defending  the rights of our children in the rights  of all citizens to live in a clean  environment we're asking all those in  power to resign and be held accountable  financially all of them we will stay  here until our demands are met  the protesters come from a cross-section  of society and that is what everyone is  telling us is so important in a city  that's been stigmatized due to a past  spillover of the Syrian war when gun  battles were fought out in the streets  the people have come together against  the ruling elite they also want to  change the sectarian system which they  accuse politicians of exploiting in  order to keep people divided but there  seems to be a stalemate now the  government isn't standing down the  people aren't leaving there are  unpredictable times ahead Stephanie  Decker al Jazeera Tripoli
 

